# Level 21, the same neighbour as `movies/level21-rescue.zmv` and the other
# player claiming it -- `a264_claim_b`, the id `$06` half of `$83:A264`.
#
# **This is the second use of the trick `movies/level21-p2-bubble.zmv` found**,
# and the round that found it is the reason this one is four lines long. The
# password is typed on port 1 exactly as every password movie types it; the
# player-select window from frame 1972 is answered on **port 2 alone**, so Zeke
# never joins and the one player on the board is Julie. `--pos` prints `--,--`
# in the first column for all 3,000 frames.
#
# Julie spawns thirty pixels east of where Zeke does -- (288,2836) against
# (258,2836) -- so the opening leg west is twenty-six frames rather than ten.
# Both end at x≈237, the neighbour's column, which is the whole reason the
# offset costs one number and not a retimed route: **a leg that ends at a wall
# survives a thirty-pixel offset.** The climb north is unchanged.
#
#   2:2700  Left twenty-six frames from (288,2836) to x=236
#   2:2726  Up the open column, into the neighbour at (237,2726)
#
# The claim lands at frame 2780 and writes:
#
#   $7E:0618  $8000   the claimant -- **not `$0006`**
#   $7E:061E  $0001   A264_EVENT_CLAIMED, the same as the first player's
#   $7E:605D  $80     the same flag byte, index 3, the same victim entry
#
# The first word is the whole difference between this movie and its sibling, and
# it is a three-byte saving in the ROM: `$83:A293  LDA #$8000` falls into
# `$83:A296  STA $18`, and id `$05` enters at the `STA` with the id still in the
# accumulator. So one player latches its own id and the other latches a bit --
# and `score_add` reads bit 15 of that word, which is why both spellings work.
#
